        "date": "2026-08-09",
        "generatedAt": "2026-08-09T11:53:26.440Z",
        "source": "claude",
        "summary": "The Strait of Hormuz remains the centre of a contested operational picture. Tehran declared the strait closed pending US compliance with a deal, per Sky News relaying an Iranian claim that is not independently corroborated. Against that, Global Times reports a Chinese maritime court settled a vessel collision dispute originating in the strait, implying physical movement of ships was occurring. The site's carrier posture assessment is a cautious closed, but the physical transit picture is contested and neither side of that dispute should be taken as settled. On the diplomatic track, multiple headlines attribute to Iranian state media a claim that Iran and Oman are near a deal, but the Iranian Foreign Minister is separately quoted saying any agreement with Oman does not constitute a reopening of the strait; these are one originating claim and one denial, not two independent data points. Iran has also published conditions for reopening, per outlets including The Telegraph. Brent crude stood at $83.55, down 0.54% over the prior 24 hours, as of 11:53 UTC; futures markets are closed and that figure is a carried-forward pre-close reading, not a live signal. Port arrivals across the Hormuz-area ports on both the Persian Gulf and Gulf of Oman sides reached 423 vessels in the most recent 24 hours. PortWatch recorded 2 transits on 2026-08-02, against a pre-crisis baseline of 73 per day; that count is seven days old and uses a different methodology from the arrivals figure. The Hormuz Index state composite sits in the extreme band, flat over 24 hours, with physical transit deviation as the top contributor; the forecast composite is in the elevated band, flat over 24 hours, driven by Polymarket escalation contracts. The two composites diverge in band, with state conditions more severe than the forward-looking signal.",
